# Supply chain

Floor controls (lockfile with new deps, no floating `latest`, no remote
`curl|sh`) live in `security-baseline`. This rule adds install and CI depth.
Full CI workflow authorship is out of scope here — keep the least-privilege
and pin floor below when a diff touches workflow YAML.

## Dependencies

- Commit the lockfile the ecosystem uses. Review the new packages in a
  dependency change, not only the direct name in the manifest
- Prefer a well-known, maintained package over a new transitive chain for the
  same job
- Pin production base images and remote installers to a literal non-`latest`
  version tag. Use a digest only when the project already digests images
- Do not pipe a remote script into a shell in a build or install step
- When a newly added package runs code at install time, stop and get explicit
  user acceptance of that package before keeping it. Reading the script alone
  is not acceptance

## CI and build

- CI secrets come from the platform secret store. Do not echo them in logs
- Do not grant a build job permission to push, deploy, or read production
  secrets unless that job's purpose is that action. Prefer least-privilege
  job defaults (`permissions:` minimal, pin action tags) when editing
  workflow YAML
- Do not claim a vulnerability scan passed unless this turn ran the scanner
  and read its result
